  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 100 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSunday through Sunday Night:\n\nModels consistently to show a deeper upper trough digging \nacross the Upper Midwest through the day Sunday. WI looks to sit\nin between two lobes of mid-level vorticity with one tracking \nacross far northern Ontario into the James Bay Region with the \nother vorticity lobe trekking across the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley and up the Ohio River Valley. While WI is trending to be \ndisplaced from the upper- level forcing along with being far \nenough north to miss out on the low-level WAA and frontogenesis \nas the accompanying surface lows look to follow mid-level \nvorticity tracks, still will need to monitor and see if there \nare any shifts in the forcing as phasing between these two \nsystems are known to shifting even a couple of days outs. \n\nAt this time, the model trends favor any precip clipping our \nsouth- southeastern part of the CWA toward the WI/IL border \nassociated with the Ohio River Valley system. If any precip does\nmake it into our area, precip type will be the main challenge. \nModels soundings favor upper-level saturation with ice in the \nDGZ, but low-level temps and moisture continue to vary. Previous\nforecast had a bit colder scenario, but the 06z-12z RRFS \nsoundings have trended warmer with above freezing temps and even\nwetbulb temps from 900mb to the surface for Sunday and lend way\nyo more of a rain setup. Meanwhile GFS has trended drier in the\nlowest levels, lining up with its south precip trend. So while \ncannot rule out some snow mixing in with this more rain leaning \ntrend, will continue to monitor to see if any shifts in the \nsystems track would bring up amounts or if the southern trend \ncontinues and bring a drier forecast. \n\nOtherwise behind this system, expect northwesterly winds and \nCAA to bring colder temps to southern WI overnight Sunday with \nlows dipping into the lower 20s. \n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nThe late weekend trough lifts out of the area for the start of \nnext week allowing cold Canadian air to dig south across the \nGreat Lakes and the rest of the Midwest. Looking at seasonal and\nnear normal temps with this CAA with highs in the 30s and lows \nin the 20s. May see a another minor mid-level trough swing \nacross the region for Tuesday, but with surface high pressure \nand limited moisture (PWATs around half an inch) not expecting \ntoo much from this mid--level disturbance other than some \nincrease cloud cover. Then midweek onward, temps are expected to\ntrend milder with high potential for above normal highs and \neven medium chance based of the EPS, GEFS, and GEPS for highs to\napproach 50F for second half of next week. This unseasonal warm\nup is attributed to upper-level ridging progged to build across\nthe region midweek onward and ahead of a potential trough over \nthe Desert Southwest as favored by WPC Cluster analysis. \nAdditionally will be monitoring the end of the week into next \nweekend to see how the aforementioned upstream trough evolves as\nit could bring a more active pattern to our neck of the woods \ngiven the signal on all the clusters, but difference in timing, \nstrength, and development remain this far out. \n\nWagner",
